    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class; the other two files are the `AcceptanceBase` helper).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the per-city capacity rule for `POST /api/owners`: creating an owner is now rejected with **409** when the target city already contains 50 or more owners.\n\nFollowing the existing OfficeFloor function-injection patterns (mirroring the sibling `CheckUniqueTelephone`/`CheckUniqueHousehold` steps), I added:\n\n1. **`CheckCityCapacity`** (`rest/function/owner/`) \u2014 new pipeline step reading the validated `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` request, counting existing owners whose city matches case-insensitively (consistent with `AssignCustomerCode`), and throwing when the count is \u2265 50.\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException`** (`rest/escalation/`) \u2014 checked exception (so it appears in the `throws` clause).\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`** \u2014 responds 409 with a `ProblemDetail` body, matching the other escalation handlers.\n4. **Escalation registration** \u2014 `officefloor/escalation/\u2026CityAtCapacityException.yml`.\n5. **Pipeline wiring** \u2014 inserted a `cityCapacity` step into `owners.POST.yml` between the household check and `build` (under `govern: [ transaction ]`), so a rejected request is never persisted.\n\nThe new step runs before `BuildOwner`/`SaveOwner`, so the incoming owner is not yet counted \u2014 the 51st create into a city filled to 50 is the one rejected, exactly as the acceptance test asserts.",
